# Cross-pattern format ancestry repair checkpoint

Status: local source correction and regression verification; independent
acceptance, full native differential acceptance and deployment remain pending.
Issue: private Decko issue 87. Baseline:
`a4cddee6e4ba69a0e65987c692ac4bdc770ea5e6`.

## Mechanism and correction

The loader selected method definitions separately for each applicable set
pattern, concatenated those filtered chains and removed duplicate owners.
Filtering discarded the body-less modules that determine where Ruby leaves a
shared ancestor. A later Right set could therefore put a shared provider
before an intervening Type override. Body, permission-helper and view-directive
lookups all use this chain mechanism.

The correction retains each token's whole module ancestry. It includes those
ancestries in general-to-specific order before selecting a view, helper or
directive. An existing shared module keeps its position, matching the retained
Ruby attachment behavior described in issue 87. The existing order for
anchorless/class-level all-set modules is preserved; no new forward include
behavior is introduced.

The fresh isolated SQLite renderer reproduces the issue at the baseline:
`B:A` instead of `B:ROOT:A`. A helper-only variant selects `true` instead of
the concrete guard's `false`; a directive variant selects `none` instead of
`update`. After correction, the renderer returns `B:ROOT:A` with call trace
`B, ROOT, A`, the restrictive helper is selected, and the directive is `update`.
The permission test also checks that the protected body is not rendered.

